# Development

Initialize with `go mod init {{project_slug}}`, then resolve the test dependency:

```sh
go get github.com/stretchr/testify
go mod tidy
make install
```

If the project chooses a remote module path, change it with `go mod edit
-module <path>` and replace `{{project_slug}}/internal/` imports in every Go
file. Install golangci-lint using its official instructions, and write its
installed version to `.golangci-lint-version`. The config's version key is
the config format, not a pinned tool release. Keep its linters when adapting
the config to a new release.

```sh
make fmt-check vet lint build test test-integration
go test -race -run TestRequestFailures ./internal/httpapi/
make coverage
```

Unit and handler tests run with the race detector; it requires cgo and a C
compiler. Tagged integration tests also build the server with `-race`.
TestMain builds once into a temporary directory. Each test starts the binary
with PORT=0, reads the actual address from JSON logs, keeps draining stdout
and kills any surviving child in cleanup. Signal cases check both SIGINT and
SIGTERM, exit status and the final stopped record.

`make fmt` applies gofmt and goimports; `make lint-fix` applies golangci-lint's
fixes. `make serve` builds `bin/server`, embeds version metadata and runs it in
the foreground. `make ci` adds coverage and vulnerability checks. A
standard-library advisory needs a patched Go toolchain, recorded in go.mod,
rather than a disabled vulnerability check.

## Background server

`make dev-bg` builds, then runs `bin/server` from `scripts/dev-bg.sh` in its
own process group, with its output in `logs/dev.log` and
`<group> <host> <port> <start time>` in `logs/dev.pid`. It returns once
`/healthz` answers (probed with `bin/server -healthcheck`), and stops the
group and prints the log's tail if that takes over 30 seconds or the script is
interrupted. A second `make dev-bg` on the same HOST and PORT reuses the
recorded server while it answers; on another port it refuses until
`make dev-stop`. A reused server keeps running the binary it started with, so
run `make dev-stop && make dev-bg` after a code change. `make dev-stop` sends
SIGTERM to the recorded group, SIGKILL after 15 seconds, and removes the pid
file. It signals nothing when the recorded pid's start time no longer matches,
since a stale pid file can name a pid another process has since taken. HOST and PORT come from the shell, as for
`make serve`; PORT=0 is refused because the health check needs a known port.

